A2: RETRIEVAL OF DOCUMENTS USING INVERTED FILES

AIM: Build an inverted index and use it to retrieve documents for a query.

CODE EXPLAINED
1. docs = {1: "...", 2: "...", ...}
   Dictionary of document id -> text. This is our collection.

2. stop_words = {...}; tokenize(text)
   tokenize lowercases the text, extracts words with regex, and drops stop words. Returns a list of terms.

3. inverted = defaultdict(dict)
   The index. Shape: inverted[term][doc_id] = term frequency. defaultdict means a new term automatically gets an empty dict.

4. Index building loop:
   for doc_id, text in docs.items():
       for term in tokenize(text):
           inverted[term][doc_id] = inverted[term].get(doc_id, 0) + 1
   For every term in every doc, add 1 to its count for that doc. Example: "documents" -> {1:1, 2:1, 4:1, 5:1}.

5. Printing the index: sorted(inverted) prints terms alphabetically with their (doc_id, tf) postings.

6. search_and(query)
   Tokenize query. Start with the doc set of the first term, then intersect (&=) with the doc set of each further term. Only docs containing ALL terms remain. inverted.get(t, {}) avoids errors for unknown terms.

7. search_ranked(query)
   For each query term, add its tf to a per-document score. Sort scores descending. A doc matching more terms/more often ranks higher. (OR semantics + tf scoring.)

8. while True loop
   Reads a query with input(), prints the AND result, the ranked list and the text of ranked docs. Type quit to exit.

EXAMPLE: query "inverted index" -> AND match [2, 4]; ranked Doc 2 and Doc 4.

VIVA Q&A
Q: What is an inverted index?
A: A map from each term to the documents containing it (postings list), like the index at the back of a book.

Q: Why "inverted"?
A: A forward index maps doc -> terms. This reverses it: term -> docs.

Q: Why is it fast?
A: Search only looks up the query terms' postings lists instead of scanning every document.

Q: What is a posting?
A: One entry (doc_id, tf). The list of all entries for a term is the postings list.

Q: How does AND work?
A: Intersection of the postings sets of all query terms.

Q: What would OR and NOT be?
A: OR = union of sets. NOT = set difference (all docs minus the term's docs).

Q: How did you rank?
A: Sum of term frequencies of query terms per doc. Simple tf scoring.

Q: What is Boolean retrieval?
A: Query with AND/OR/NOT; a doc either matches or not, no ranking.

Q: Data structure used?
A: defaultdict of dicts (hash-based dictionary). Average O(1) lookup.

Q: What is TF-IDF?
A: tf = count in doc; idf = log(N/df). High weight for terms frequent in a doc but rare in the collection.

Q: How can you improve it?
A: TF-IDF weights, positional index for phrase queries, stemming, postings compression, sorted postings for faster merge.

Q: Time complexity of intersecting two postings lists?
A: O(m + n) if sorted lists are merged; here set intersection is about O(min(m, n)).

SHORT VIVA ANSWER
"This program builds an inverted index that maps every term to the documents containing it along with the term frequency. A query is answered by looking up the query terms in the index: AND search intersects the document sets, and ranked search sums the term frequencies so the best matching documents come first."

EXTRA QUESTIONS
Q: What is the difference between precision and recall? A: Precision = relevant retrieved / all retrieved. Recall = relevant retrieved / all relevant documents.
Q: What is stemming vs lemmatization? A: Stemming crudely chops word endings (running->run). Lemmatization uses a dictionary to get the base form (better->good). Lemmatization is more accurate.
Q: What is the IR pipeline? A: Crawl -> pre-process -> index -> query processing -> ranking -> evaluation.
